WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.335 --> 00:00:03.586
- This is what the future

00:00:03.586 --> 00:00:06.840
of genetic engineering
looks like.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.467
With advanced
DNA editing techniques,

00:00:09.467 --> 00:00:13.513
vaccines for deadly infectious
diseases with no side effects

00:00:13.513 --> 00:00:16.099
are developed
in a matter of weeks.

00:00:16.099 --> 00:00:20.270
Genes from any plant or animal
can be combined to create

00:00:20.270 --> 00:00:24.566
new hybrid organisms
resilient to climate change.

00:00:24.566 --> 00:00:28.111
This technique can even
restore extinct species.

00:00:28.111 --> 00:00:32.782
Doctors can detect every known
genetic disorder early on

00:00:32.782 --> 00:00:34.909
and repair mutations quickly,

00:00:34.909 --> 00:00:39.372
giving children
happy and healthy lives.

00:00:39.372 --> 00:00:41.291
- Our environmental clock
is ticking.

00:00:41.291 --> 00:00:45.128
Today, scientists are blazing
a trail to this very future.

00:00:45.128 --> 00:00:46.713
- Tinkering with genes is

00:00:46.713 --> 00:00:48.882
actually an ethical thing
to do.

00:00:48.882 --> 00:00:52.010
- I want to know what
breakthroughs are being made.

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:54.804
- Technologies like CRISPR
are incredibly powerful.

00:00:54.804 --> 00:00:56.306
- What we're doing here
is really

00:00:56.306 --> 00:00:58.516
the beginning
of a true revolution.

00:00:58.516 --> 00:01:00.477
- That will forge
the future to...

00:01:00.477 --> 00:01:02.062
That is incredible.

00:01:02.062 --> 00:01:04.689
The rise of
genetic engineering.

00:01:04.689 --> 00:01:07.692
[dramatic music]

00:01:07.692 --> 00:01:14.616
♪ ♪

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:42.060
My name is Kondwani Phiri.
I'm a genetic researcher.

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:44.896
You can say it's in my blood.

00:01:44.896 --> 00:01:47.857
From an early age of
about eight or nine or so,

00:01:47.857 --> 00:01:51.694
I had just a natural curiosity
for the natural world.

00:01:51.694 --> 00:01:53.196
Cancer runs in my family.

00:01:53.196 --> 00:01:55.782
In fact, I lost my aunt
to breast cancer.

00:01:55.782 --> 00:01:59.410
And that piqued my interest
in genetics.

00:01:59.410 --> 00:02:02.664
I'm fascinated by
how the genes we inherit

00:02:02.664 --> 00:02:06.501
shape our biological destiny,
including our health.

00:02:06.501 --> 00:02:08.545
I believe the key
to treatment and cures

00:02:08.545 --> 00:02:10.046
for diseases like these

00:02:10.046 --> 00:02:14.259
lies in explicit efforts
to engineer human genes.

00:02:14.259 --> 00:02:16.886
But can we fix
not only what's broken,

00:02:16.886 --> 00:02:19.097
but also enhance
our genetic codes

00:02:19.097 --> 00:02:21.141
and improve on Mother Nature?

00:02:21.141 --> 00:02:23.309
Someday, will humans direct

00:02:23.309 --> 00:02:26.938
their own evolution,
and should we?

00:02:26.938 --> 00:02:28.690
[inquisitive music]

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:31.276
I'm starting my journey
in Washington D.C.

00:02:31.276 --> 00:02:33.278
to visit a family
who's grappling

00:02:33.278 --> 00:02:36.489
with the difficulties
caused by genetic disease.

00:02:36.489 --> 00:02:39.033
Hello!
Hi, Annabel.

00:02:39.033 --> 00:02:41.995
- Can you shake hands, Annabel?
- Hi.

00:02:41.995 --> 00:02:43.955
Oh, my gosh.
How are you?

00:02:43.955 --> 00:02:46.457
Oh, I think she's shy.
[chuckles]

00:02:46.457 --> 00:02:48.459
Four-year-old Annabel Frost

00:02:48.459 --> 00:02:50.920
has a heart-wrenching
genetic disorder.

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:52.922
Could you tell me
a little bit about Annabel?

00:02:52.922 --> 00:02:55.884
- When Annabel was born,
it was such a wonderful moment.

00:02:55.884 --> 00:02:59.179
But we were in this happy fog
and didn't notice

00:02:59.179 --> 00:03:03.391
the nystagmus,
the eye movements.

00:03:03.391 --> 00:03:05.560
We didn't notice
the jerky muscle movements.

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:07.020
We didn't notice any of that.

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:09.272
But the doctors realized
that something was wrong.

00:03:09.272 --> 00:03:11.107
- They diagnosed Annabel with

00:03:11.107 --> 00:03:14.152
alternating hemiplegia
of childhood, or AHC.

00:03:14.152 --> 00:03:15.486
- Hi.

00:03:15.486 --> 00:03:17.322
- This extremely rare disorder

00:03:17.322 --> 00:03:20.241
causes debilitating spells
of paralysis,

00:03:20.241 --> 00:03:24.829
delayed development,
and life-threatening seizures.

00:03:24.829 --> 00:03:26.623
- She'd have days of paralysis

00:03:26.623 --> 00:03:28.249
where her whole
one side of her body,

00:03:28.249 --> 00:03:32.170
she'd drag it along
behind her as she crawled.

00:03:32.170 --> 00:03:35.506
- You know, once a day she will
have these choking episodes

00:03:35.506 --> 00:03:37.675
where I need to pick her up
and hit her on the back

00:03:37.675 --> 00:03:40.845
to make sure
that she can breathe.

00:03:42.597 --> 00:03:43.890
- Annabel's condition

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:46.768
is a result of a problem
in her DNA.

00:03:46.768 --> 00:03:49.771
The twisting double helix
of DNA is made up of

00:03:49.771 --> 00:03:53.024
four different base pairs
or nucleotides.

00:03:53.024 --> 00:03:55.568
The unique sequence
of these base pairs

00:03:55.568 --> 00:03:58.988
are instructions for the body
to make proteins.

00:03:58.988 --> 00:04:01.324
The base pairs are like
musical notes in a score

00:04:01.324 --> 00:04:04.118
that tell a musician
how to play a song.

00:04:04.118 --> 00:04:05.912
When all
the different sections

00:04:05.912 --> 00:04:07.413
of the DNA are played,

00:04:07.413 --> 00:04:10.250
the result is
a biological symphony.

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:12.293
[lively classical music]

00:04:12.293 --> 00:04:14.587
But like a musical note
played out of key

00:04:14.587 --> 00:04:17.215
that compromises
the entire performance,

00:04:17.215 --> 00:04:20.009
there is an error
in one of Annabel's genes.

00:04:20.009 --> 00:04:22.178
- [cries]

00:04:22.178 --> 00:04:25.014
- The mutation
in the ATP1A3 gene

00:04:25.014 --> 00:04:27.141
is disrupting her nerve cells,

00:04:27.141 --> 00:04:29.686
leading to
her severe condition.

00:04:29.686 --> 00:04:32.355
AHC is
fundamentally intertwined

00:04:32.355 --> 00:04:33.940
in her genetic code.

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:37.777
There is no restorative
treatment or cure.

00:04:37.777 --> 00:04:40.989
- The medicines that they have
are kind of more about

00:04:40.989 --> 00:04:46.369
masking symptoms in a very
unspecific, untargeted way.

00:04:46.369 --> 00:04:48.997
- And we wanted to try to
figure out if there was a way

00:04:48.997 --> 00:04:52.208
to address the cause
and not the symptoms.

00:04:52.208 --> 00:04:57.255
- Without targeted treatment,
Annabel could die any day.

00:04:57.255 --> 00:05:00.425
both: ♪ Happy birthday
dear Annabel ♪

00:05:00.425 --> 00:05:02.176
- There's a timeline
on this for us.

00:05:02.176 --> 00:05:03.511
We've got a ticking clock

00:05:03.511 --> 00:05:05.805
in the back of our minds
all the time.

00:05:05.805 --> 00:05:10.184
- The Frosts are desperate
for any kind of help.

00:05:10.184 --> 00:05:13.396
From sickle cell anemia
to Huntington's disease,

00:05:13.396 --> 00:05:15.189
millions of people suffer from

00:05:15.189 --> 00:05:18.693
thousands of genetic disorders
much like Annabel's.

00:05:18.693 --> 00:05:22.238
Unless scientists can address
these genetic errors,

00:05:22.238 --> 00:05:25.575
children like Annabel will
suffer agonizing pain,

00:05:25.575 --> 00:05:27.618
and some will die young.

00:05:27.618 --> 00:05:30.121
I want to know,
can genetic engineering

00:05:30.121 --> 00:05:33.416
help families like this
in the future?

00:05:33.416 --> 00:05:35.418
Oh, my God.
- Oh, that's so nice.

00:05:35.418 --> 00:05:37.211
[dynamic music]

00:05:37.211 --> 00:05:39.172
- The double helix structure
of DNA

00:05:39.172 --> 00:05:42.717
was first revealed in 1953
by Rosalind Franklin,

00:05:42.717 --> 00:05:45.470
Francis Crick,
and James Watson.

00:05:45.470 --> 00:05:48.431
And within a few decades,
scientists discovered

00:05:48.431 --> 00:05:52.185
how to alter the DNA
of living organisms.

00:05:52.185 --> 00:05:55.605
But gene editing
really took off around 2013

00:05:55.605 --> 00:05:58.399
when scientists harnessed
a molecular mechanism

00:05:58.399 --> 00:06:00.443
found in certain bacteria.

00:06:00.443 --> 00:06:04.197
This remarkable mechanism
is called CRISPR-Cas9,

00:06:04.197 --> 00:06:05.948
or CRISPR for short.

00:06:05.948 --> 00:06:09.327
CRISPR-Cas9 contains an enzyme
that serves as the bacteria's

00:06:09.327 --> 00:06:11.829
defense mechanism
against viruses.

00:06:11.829 --> 00:06:14.123
When viruses
infect the bacteria,

00:06:14.123 --> 00:06:17.752
the CRISPR machinery targets
a specific section of DNA.

00:06:17.752 --> 00:06:20.797
Then the enzyme responds
like a pair of scissors

00:06:20.797 --> 00:06:23.424
and literally cuts the virus'
genetic code apart.

00:06:23.424 --> 00:06:26.302
Scientists are now harnessing
this natural mechanism

00:06:26.302 --> 00:06:30.390
to target and precisely edit
the DNA of numerous organisms.

00:06:31.933 --> 00:06:33.810
To find out how,

00:06:33.810 --> 00:06:37.563
I'm heading to a biotech lab
in Brooklyn, New York.

00:06:37.563 --> 00:06:39.315
I'm on my way to Genspace,

00:06:39.315 --> 00:06:43.027
which is the world's first
community biology lab.

00:06:43.027 --> 00:06:47.407
Beth Tuck is the Director of
Science Education at Genspace.

00:06:47.407 --> 00:06:50.868
- We are an open-access science
lab or a community biology lab.

00:06:50.868 --> 00:06:53.579
- So are you telling me
if I'm a computer programmer

00:06:53.579 --> 00:06:55.498
and I wanted to learn
more about biology,

00:06:55.498 --> 00:06:58.000
I could essentially
come down here and take a class

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:00.795
and use your facility?
- That's it.

00:07:00.795 --> 00:07:03.631
- Beth is going to show me
how to use CRISPR in the lab

00:07:03.631 --> 00:07:07.385
to edit a bacteria's genes
in new and novel ways.

00:07:07.385 --> 00:07:09.178
I brought this kit.

00:07:09.178 --> 00:07:13.099
Could you explain a little bit
how this kit works?

00:07:13.099 --> 00:07:15.226
- Yeah, sure.
Got a pipette.

00:07:15.226 --> 00:07:17.270
This is our measuring device.

00:07:17.270 --> 00:07:20.231
Got a rack where we'll put
some of our plastic tubes,

00:07:20.231 --> 00:07:23.985
some plates, petri dishes,
and then our E. coli.

00:07:23.985 --> 00:07:26.404
- E. coli is
a type of bacteria

00:07:26.404 --> 00:07:29.031
that lives in the intestines
of humans and animals.

00:07:29.031 --> 00:07:33.870
Though some strains can be
deadly, most are harmless.

00:07:33.870 --> 00:07:36.831
Gloved up.
Let's do this.

00:07:36.831 --> 00:07:40.001
- All right, so we've got
our E. coli here.

00:07:40.001 --> 00:07:43.171
These are called DH5-Alpha,
which is a type of species

00:07:43.171 --> 00:07:45.840
that isn't harmful to people
with normal immune systems.

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:47.300
- That's good to know.

00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:49.093
- So our first thing
that we need to do

00:07:49.093 --> 00:07:52.013
is actually get these bacteria
out of this jar.

00:07:52.013 --> 00:07:54.015
And so you just kind of
scoop it in there--yup.

00:07:54.015 --> 00:07:56.476
swirl it around,
bring a little bit up.

00:07:56.476 --> 00:07:57.852
The way that
this experiment works

00:07:57.852 --> 00:08:02.607
is we're gonna spend a day
to prepare the bacteria.

00:08:02.607 --> 00:08:05.485
- Next, we insert
a CRISPR construct

00:08:05.485 --> 00:08:07.153
that will alter their DNA.

00:08:07.153 --> 00:08:09.864
- So we're going to add in
the CRISPR machinery

00:08:09.864 --> 00:08:12.742
and then change the cells.
- Ah.

00:08:12.742 --> 00:08:15.495
The CRISPR machinery
targets and edits

00:08:15.495 --> 00:08:17.914
a specific gene
in the E. coli,

00:08:17.914 --> 00:08:21.250
so that it can adapt to
a lethal dose of antibiotics.

00:08:21.250 --> 00:08:23.961
Normally, bacteria can't grow

00:08:23.961 --> 00:08:25.922
in the presence
of this antibiotic.

00:08:25.922 --> 00:08:27.465
The drug kills the bacteria.

00:08:27.465 --> 00:08:30.218
- We need to get
these bacteria onto this plate

00:08:30.218 --> 00:08:31.844
so they can grow.

00:08:31.844 --> 00:08:33.804
You're just gonna
gently drag it

00:08:33.804 --> 00:08:35.348
across the surface
of the plate.

00:08:35.348 --> 00:08:37.225
- That's it?
- Yeah, that's it.

00:08:37.225 --> 00:08:39.560
- Over the course of
several days,

00:08:39.560 --> 00:08:42.271
Beth grows
the gene-edited bacteria

00:08:42.271 --> 00:08:43.856
in a petri dish laced with

00:08:43.856 --> 00:08:46.067
the E. coli-killing
antibiotic,

00:08:46.067 --> 00:08:48.486
but despite
this toxic onslaught,

00:08:48.486 --> 00:08:52.782
the genetically altered
E. coli continued to grow.

00:08:52.782 --> 00:08:55.535
I see plenty of growth.

00:08:55.535 --> 00:08:57.328
- The whole point of this
is to show that

00:08:57.328 --> 00:09:00.289
changing an organism's DNA
can change its features,

00:09:00.289 --> 00:09:02.375
and that you can do it
with CRISPR

00:09:02.375 --> 00:09:03.626
in a really precise way,

00:09:03.626 --> 00:09:06.045
in a way that wasn't
feasible before.

00:09:06.045 --> 00:09:07.421
- What kind of
positive outcomes

00:09:07.421 --> 00:09:09.715
can we expect
from using CRISPR?

00:09:09.715 --> 00:09:11.676
- You can use it to design

00:09:11.676 --> 00:09:15.304
new on-the-spot testing
for infectious diseases.

00:09:15.304 --> 00:09:19.725
You can use it to cut out
HIV from human cells.

00:09:19.725 --> 00:09:20.935
There are so many more uses

00:09:20.935 --> 00:09:22.728
that we haven't
even imagined yet.

00:09:22.728 --> 00:09:25.398
That, to me, is why
this stuff is so exciting.

00:09:25.398 --> 00:09:27.984
- Harnessing the natural
ability of CRISPR

00:09:27.984 --> 00:09:31.112
and transforming it
into a technological tool

00:09:31.112 --> 00:09:35.324
has the potential to address
all sorts of problems.

00:09:37.368 --> 00:09:40.204
- In the future, advanced
gene editing techniques

00:09:40.204 --> 00:09:43.916
are reworking microbes
to create healthier lives.

00:09:43.916 --> 00:09:47.587
Bacteria are engineered into
many pharmaceutical factories.

00:09:47.587 --> 00:09:51.007
These microorganisms
generate an inexpensive,

00:09:51.007 --> 00:09:52.883
abundant supply of medicines

00:09:52.883 --> 00:09:57.805
for diseases like malaria,
rabies, and coronavirus.

00:09:57.805 --> 00:09:59.682
And specially modified yeast

00:09:59.682 --> 00:10:02.935
converts organic waste
into green bio fuels.

00:10:02.935 --> 00:10:06.188
This innovative energy source
is not only renewable,

00:10:06.188 --> 00:10:08.149
but it also packs
more of a punch

00:10:08.149 --> 00:10:10.776
than regular fossil fuels.

00:10:10.776 --> 00:10:13.446
[light dramatic music]

00:10:13.446 --> 00:10:16.324
- Formulated by Charles
Darwin, natural selection

00:10:16.324 --> 00:10:19.035
is the driving force
of evolution.

00:10:19.035 --> 00:10:22.705
While many individuals perish,
the species best adapted

00:10:22.705 --> 00:10:25.875
to their environment
survive and reproduce,

00:10:25.875 --> 00:10:28.461
passing along their
advantageous genetic traits

00:10:28.461 --> 00:10:30.129
to their offspring.

00:10:30.129 --> 00:10:33.758
These traits like height,
eye color, strength,

00:10:33.758 --> 00:10:37.219
and even personality
are contained in genes.

00:10:37.219 --> 00:10:40.431
Over generations, the process
of natural selection

00:10:40.431 --> 00:10:44.644
perpetually fine tunes the
species at the genetic level,

00:10:44.644 --> 00:10:46.896
helping individuals
to continually adapt

00:10:46.896 --> 00:10:50.941
to changes
in their environment.

00:10:50.941 --> 00:10:53.736
Humans learned to direct
this evolutionary process

00:10:53.736 --> 00:10:56.447
through artificial selection.

00:10:56.447 --> 00:10:58.616
Through the intentional
selection of mates,

00:10:58.616 --> 00:11:00.785
humans facilitated
the breeding

00:11:00.785 --> 00:11:04.997
of animal offspring
with desired characteristics.

00:11:04.997 --> 00:11:07.583
Arguably,
the first known example

00:11:07.583 --> 00:11:09.251
of artificial selection
happened

00:11:09.251 --> 00:11:11.754
more than 15,000 years ago

00:11:11.754 --> 00:11:13.756
when humans
domesticated the wolf,

00:11:13.756 --> 00:11:16.967
breeding what we now
know today as the dog.

00:11:16.967 --> 00:11:18.761
♪ ♪

00:11:18.761 --> 00:11:22.014
In agriculture, artificial
selection has improved

00:11:22.014 --> 00:11:25.518
and even created
new fruits and vegetables.

00:11:25.518 --> 00:11:28.521
Through selective breeding
for certain traits,

00:11:28.521 --> 00:11:31.399
the simple wild mustard plant
was transformed

00:11:31.399 --> 00:11:35.152
into cauliflower, cabbage,
and even broccoli.

00:11:35.152 --> 00:11:36.946
But even artificial selection

00:11:36.946 --> 00:11:39.615
takes at least
a few generations to work.

00:11:39.615 --> 00:11:42.535
Capitalizing on
the natural CRISPR process,

00:11:42.535 --> 00:11:44.829
gene editing in the lab

00:11:44.829 --> 00:11:48.374
can compress evolution
down to a matter of months.

00:11:48.374 --> 00:11:51.043
Using CRISPR to
manually engineer DNA

00:11:51.043 --> 00:11:54.296
is the next step
in accelerated evolution.

00:11:54.296 --> 00:11:56.006
And with this remarkable tool,

00:11:56.006 --> 00:12:00.219
scientists can even improve
our food supply.

00:12:00.219 --> 00:12:03.347
This matters,
because over 10% of the world

00:12:03.347 --> 00:12:06.183
suffers from hunger
and malnourishment.

00:12:06.183 --> 00:12:07.768
I'm in Raleigh,
North Carolina,

00:12:07.768 --> 00:12:09.895
where pioneering
food scientist

00:12:09.895 --> 00:12:12.940
Dr. Rodolphe Barrangou
is using gene editing

00:12:12.940 --> 00:12:16.193
to boost food production
and feed the planet.

00:12:16.193 --> 00:12:17.486
- We're going to be able to

00:12:17.486 --> 00:12:19.572
breed crops that are
more efficient,

00:12:19.572 --> 00:12:22.199
that are more resistant
to disease.

00:12:22.199 --> 00:12:24.618
The biggest impact CRISPR
may have short-term,

00:12:24.618 --> 00:12:26.579
maybe the next decade or so,

00:12:26.579 --> 00:12:28.914
will to be to revolutionize
the food supply chain.

00:12:28.914 --> 00:12:31.959
- In fact, Rodolphe had
a hand in the discovery

00:12:31.959 --> 00:12:34.170
of the natural CRISPR
mechanism in bacteria.

00:12:34.170 --> 00:12:37.006
And his use of CRISPR
as a technology in milk

00:12:37.006 --> 00:12:40.509
is leading to remarkable
innovations in dairy products.

00:12:40.509 --> 00:12:42.511
- So what we're looking
at here is milk

00:12:42.511 --> 00:12:44.513
that is in the process
of fermenting.

00:12:44.513 --> 00:12:46.474
- Okay, so this is in
the earlier stage of things?

00:12:46.474 --> 00:12:48.017
- Very early stage.

00:12:48.017 --> 00:12:51.061
So we add the bacteria to start
the fermentation process

00:12:51.061 --> 00:12:52.354
and then use the lactose

00:12:52.354 --> 00:12:55.149
to solidify milk
into cheese and yogurt.

00:12:55.149 --> 00:12:59.028
- In most dairy facilities,
regular bacteria and yeast

00:12:59.028 --> 00:13:02.072
kickstart
the fermentation process.

00:13:02.072 --> 00:13:04.575
But before starting,
Rodolphe added a twist.

00:13:04.575 --> 00:13:06.410
He used CRISPR technology

00:13:06.410 --> 00:13:10.623
to enhance the bacteria used
in the fermentation of milk.

00:13:10.623 --> 00:13:13.459
- We used it to build
just instant bacteria

00:13:13.459 --> 00:13:16.295
to make cheese and yogurt,
to have better fermentations

00:13:16.295 --> 00:13:18.923
and better manufacturing
of dairy products.

00:13:18.923 --> 00:13:21.008
- By vaccinating the bacteria,

00:13:21.008 --> 00:13:23.677
his dairy cultures
almost always succeed,

00:13:23.677 --> 00:13:27.598
which improves the process
of making yogurt and cheese.

00:13:27.598 --> 00:13:30.434
Processes like these
also make them healthier.

00:13:30.434 --> 00:13:31.769
- CRISPR has been
a life changer.

00:13:31.769 --> 00:13:34.271
And it also has opened
tremendous avenues

00:13:34.271 --> 00:13:36.565
to provide a healthier
and more sustainable

00:13:36.565 --> 00:13:37.775
food supply for humanity.

00:13:37.775 --> 00:13:39.527
- But the true revolutionary

00:13:39.527 --> 00:13:41.445
potential of CRISPR technology

00:13:41.445 --> 00:13:45.199
is just now starting
to be realized.

00:13:45.199 --> 00:13:48.869
- Other people caught on to
using these molecular machines

00:13:48.869 --> 00:13:51.705
to actually cut DNA
to do genome editing,

00:13:51.705 --> 00:13:56.126
not just in bacteria,
but in other organisms.

00:13:56.126 --> 00:13:59.213
By understanding what CRISPR
is and how it works,

00:13:59.213 --> 00:14:02.007
scientists were able to
develop technologies

00:14:02.007 --> 00:14:04.134
that enable us to
now change the world.

00:14:04.134 --> 00:14:05.553
[bright electronic music]

00:14:05.553 --> 00:14:07.596
- By 2050,
the world population

00:14:07.596 --> 00:14:10.975
is expected to soar
to 10 billion people.

00:14:10.975 --> 00:14:15.271
Food production will need to
increase 70% to catch up.

00:14:15.271 --> 00:14:17.064
To feed our growing population

00:14:17.064 --> 00:14:19.149
with the same amount
of farm land,

00:14:19.149 --> 00:14:22.987
the mass production of
food must be hyper efficient.

00:14:22.987 --> 00:14:26.156
At NC State Plants
and Microbial Biology Lab,

00:14:26.156 --> 00:14:29.076
Rodolphe's colleague,
Mary Beth Dallas,

00:14:29.076 --> 00:14:31.579
is facing
this daunting prospect.

00:14:31.579 --> 00:14:36.792
- I manage this lab, and I
also do research on cassava.

00:14:36.792 --> 00:14:39.169
- Also known as
the yucca plant,

00:14:39.169 --> 00:14:41.380
cassava is
the primary food staple

00:14:41.380 --> 00:14:43.674
of nearly one billion people.

00:14:43.674 --> 00:14:46.886
- Cassava plant
is very close to my heart.

00:14:46.886 --> 00:14:49.471
- This root vegetable
is kind of like a potato,

00:14:49.471 --> 00:14:53.767
and is widely grown across
Africa and the Americas.

00:14:53.767 --> 00:14:57.521
- They can actually harvest
the tubers and make flour,

00:14:57.521 --> 00:14:59.523
and they can make
breads out of that.

00:14:59.523 --> 00:15:03.777
They can also eat the greens.
It's really a nice plant.

00:15:03.777 --> 00:15:06.363
- Yeah, and me being
a native Zambian--

00:15:06.363 --> 00:15:07.865
that's where I was born.

00:15:07.865 --> 00:15:10.951
I grew up eating cassava as
well as cassava leaves, so...

00:15:10.951 --> 00:15:12.578
- You know all about it.
- I know all about it.

00:15:12.578 --> 00:15:15.080
It has a special place
in my heart as well too.

00:15:15.080 --> 00:15:17.541
But there's a problem.

00:15:17.541 --> 00:15:20.044
A malady called
cassava mosaic disease

00:15:20.044 --> 00:15:22.713
is ravaging
this critical food source.

00:15:22.713 --> 00:15:25.799
In Africa alone,
it's destroyed cassava crops,

00:15:25.799 --> 00:15:28.010
leading to numerous famines.

00:15:28.010 --> 00:15:32.598
- You can see here
the devastation of the plants.

00:15:32.598 --> 00:15:38.020
It gets really thin leaves,
and a mosaic pattern occurs.

00:15:38.020 --> 00:15:40.064
When the leaves
get destroyed like this,

00:15:40.064 --> 00:15:42.274
they cannot
photosynthesize properly

00:15:42.274 --> 00:15:44.526
and the tubers
that are under the ground

00:15:44.526 --> 00:15:48.030
cannot get the right nutrients,
and they get all shriveled,

00:15:48.030 --> 00:15:51.033
and then you can't use
the tubers for the food.

00:15:51.033 --> 00:15:55.287
We want to combat
and try to find a way

00:15:55.287 --> 00:15:58.916
to stop the devastation
of these crops.

00:15:58.916 --> 00:16:00.334
- To wage this war,

00:16:00.334 --> 00:16:03.087
Mary's lab must take
an experimental approach.

00:16:03.087 --> 00:16:05.923
[energetic music]

00:16:05.923 --> 00:16:07.841
Using what's called
a gene gun,

00:16:07.841 --> 00:16:12.096
CRISPR-altered DNA is injected
into the cassava plant.

00:16:12.096 --> 00:16:16.684
- We bombard the stems
with the CRISPR construct,

00:16:16.684 --> 00:16:18.185
and what happens

00:16:18.185 --> 00:16:20.354
is the leaves grow up.
- Ah.

00:16:20.354 --> 00:16:21.814
In principle,

00:16:21.814 --> 00:16:24.024
as the leaves and the tubers
grow from the stem,

00:16:24.024 --> 00:16:25.734
the plant will become
more resistant

00:16:25.734 --> 00:16:27.903
to the crippling
mosaic disease.

00:16:27.903 --> 00:16:30.656
When can we see
a possible usage

00:16:30.656 --> 00:16:33.784
in African countries
that are afflicted?

00:16:33.784 --> 00:16:36.829
- We're still trying to hone in
that technique.

00:16:36.829 --> 00:16:38.622
So hopefully,
we'll get it soon.

00:16:38.622 --> 00:16:41.041
- Hopefully soon.
[laughter]

00:16:41.041 --> 00:16:44.795
Imagine--gene editing
could help end world hunger.

00:16:44.795 --> 00:16:47.548
- This is also applicable
beyond crops

00:16:47.548 --> 00:16:49.550
to things like trees.

00:16:49.550 --> 00:16:52.344
Forests may be the biggest
farms that we have.

00:16:52.344 --> 00:16:55.180
- But as
the human population grows,

00:16:55.180 --> 00:16:57.933
so do our agricultural needs.

00:16:57.933 --> 00:16:59.768
This leads to deforestation,

00:16:59.768 --> 00:17:02.938
which is one of the primary
causes of climate change,

00:17:02.938 --> 00:17:04.898
contributing to
the record high temperatures

00:17:04.898 --> 00:17:06.984
we see today.

00:17:06.984 --> 00:17:08.861
To reverse this scary trend,

00:17:08.861 --> 00:17:11.655
one solution is to plant
more trees to absorb

00:17:11.655 --> 00:17:15.659
greenhouse gases
like carbon dioxide.

00:17:15.659 --> 00:17:19.413
Rodolphe is collaborating with
tree biologist Dr. Jack Wang

00:17:19.413 --> 00:17:23.417
to grow more trees, and fast.

00:17:23.417 --> 00:17:25.210
What do we have here?

00:17:25.210 --> 00:17:28.130
- So these are
transgenic trees.

00:17:28.130 --> 00:17:29.923
- Jack's lab has created over

00:17:29.923 --> 00:17:33.343
10,000 types of
genetically modified trees.

00:17:33.343 --> 00:17:36.180
The goal is to optimize traits
for different industries,

00:17:36.180 --> 00:17:37.806
like timber or paper,

00:17:37.806 --> 00:17:40.517
to reduce their
environmental footprint.

00:17:40.517 --> 00:17:44.396
- We deliver CRISPR
into these cells.

00:17:44.396 --> 00:17:46.940
- Once CRISPR has
altered the tree DNA,

00:17:46.940 --> 00:17:50.069
the embryos then grow
enhanced roots and shoots.

00:17:50.069 --> 00:17:52.529
- so these have been
engineered precisely

00:17:52.529 --> 00:17:55.949
for a specific genetic change
using CRISPR.

00:17:55.949 --> 00:17:57.534
- This helps Jack
to select for

00:17:57.534 --> 00:18:00.287
specific traits more quickly
and efficiently

00:18:00.287 --> 00:18:02.331
than traditional
plant breeding.

00:18:02.331 --> 00:18:05.000
- So this little bit
of seedling is now

00:18:05.000 --> 00:18:09.088
a tiny little CRISPR-edited
forest tree species.

00:18:09.088 --> 00:18:10.380
- This is at the stage

00:18:10.380 --> 00:18:12.257
just before it goes
into the greenhouse?

00:18:12.257 --> 00:18:13.842
- That's right.
So it's now ready

00:18:13.842 --> 00:18:15.761
to be grown for
five to six months.

00:18:15.761 --> 00:18:18.263
- Fantastic.
So cool.

00:18:18.263 --> 00:18:19.765
Jack's next step

00:18:19.765 --> 00:18:21.975
is taking these seedlings
over to the greenhouse

00:18:21.975 --> 00:18:23.977
where they'll be fully grown
and studied.

00:18:23.977 --> 00:18:26.396
- Compared to
15, 20 years it takes

00:18:26.396 --> 00:18:29.525
to breed a tree
in a natural population,

00:18:29.525 --> 00:18:33.153
in the greenhouse setting,
we can analyze and produce

00:18:33.153 --> 00:18:34.571
new genetically improved trees

00:18:34.571 --> 00:18:36.281
in as little as
five to six months.

00:18:36.281 --> 00:18:39.535
- That is incredible.
Six months?

00:18:39.535 --> 00:18:41.703
Here they're quickly
growing poplar trees,

00:18:41.703 --> 00:18:43.664
which is the most
effective species

00:18:43.664 --> 00:18:46.250
at absorbing carbon dioxide
from the air.

00:18:46.250 --> 00:18:47.626
- Yeah, they can capture

00:18:47.626 --> 00:18:50.754
a very large amount of carbon
from the atmosphere,

00:18:50.754 --> 00:18:53.632
and we have to start solving
the problem

00:18:53.632 --> 00:18:54.967
right here, right now.

00:18:54.967 --> 00:18:59.304
We cannot afford to wait
for another 20 or 30 years.

00:18:59.304 --> 00:19:01.598
- This technique
for breeding trees

00:19:01.598 --> 00:19:05.686
has given reforestation
efforts a major jump-start.

00:19:05.686 --> 00:19:07.521
Our environmental clock
is ticking,

00:19:07.521 --> 00:19:09.481
and if we want
to create a better,

00:19:09.481 --> 00:19:11.942
healthier environment
for future generations,

00:19:11.942 --> 00:19:14.444
it's something that
has to be done now.

00:19:14.444 --> 00:19:15.779
- What we're doing here

00:19:15.779 --> 00:19:18.407
is really the beginning
of a true revolution.

00:19:18.407 --> 00:19:22.035
The next green revolution,
coming to a forest near you.

00:19:22.035 --> 00:19:23.745
- Yes.

00:19:23.745 --> 00:19:25.789
- I think it's going to
revolutionize our world

00:19:25.789 --> 00:19:29.209
and solve the grand challenges
that we have on the planet.

00:19:29.209 --> 00:19:31.837
- A new CRISPR-fueled
green revolution

00:19:31.837 --> 00:19:34.715
will forge a path
to a more bountiful world.

00:19:34.715 --> 00:19:37.134
[dramatic music]

00:19:38.468 --> 00:19:41.054
- In the future,
gene edited plants

00:19:41.054 --> 00:19:44.683
are addressing world hunger
and climate change.

00:19:44.683 --> 00:19:47.811
Enhanced food crops
grow in harsh conditions,

00:19:47.811 --> 00:19:50.314
even in water-parched deserts.

00:19:50.314 --> 00:19:53.025
Thanks to these
disease-resistant plants,

00:19:53.025 --> 00:19:56.069
famines across the world
are a thing of the past.

00:19:56.069 --> 00:19:59.573
New fast-growing forests
collectively take in

00:19:59.573 --> 00:20:02.659
the excess carbon dioxide
from the atmosphere,

00:20:02.659 --> 00:20:05.287
cooling the climate
and restoring balance

00:20:05.287 --> 00:20:07.539
to the world's ecosystem.

00:20:08.665 --> 00:20:11.001
[inquisitive music]

00:20:11.001 --> 00:20:13.962
- As I see it,
gene editing is both a faster

00:20:13.962 --> 00:20:17.841
and more precise method
for artificial selection.

00:20:17.841 --> 00:20:20.052
While it works in
microbes and plants,

00:20:20.052 --> 00:20:22.221
how feasible is gene editing

00:20:22.221 --> 00:20:26.433
in more biologically complex
organisms like animals?

00:20:26.433 --> 00:20:28.435
I'm in Davis, California,

00:20:28.435 --> 00:20:30.812
to meet geneticist
Alison Van Eenennaam.

00:20:30.812 --> 00:20:32.689
She's a pioneer in this field,

00:20:32.689 --> 00:20:36.526
and I'm getting
acquainted with her work.

00:20:36.526 --> 00:20:38.904
Oh, no.

00:20:38.904 --> 00:20:41.198
- Scientists tend to be
problem solvers,

00:20:41.198 --> 00:20:42.991
and want to try
and address problems

00:20:42.991 --> 00:20:45.702
using the best method they can,

00:20:45.702 --> 00:20:48.747
and my lab is trying to breed
better cattle.

00:20:48.747 --> 00:20:51.833
- There are about
1 billion cattle on earth.

00:20:51.833 --> 00:20:54.002
That's a lot of animals
to manage,

00:20:54.002 --> 00:20:56.338
but Alison is making
cattle farms safer

00:20:56.338 --> 00:20:58.674
by eliminating
one particular trait.

00:20:58.674 --> 00:21:01.260
- I see some horns up here.
- Okay.

00:21:01.260 --> 00:21:03.053
- Dairy cows have been
bred to be

00:21:03.053 --> 00:21:04.805
very optimal
for dairy production,

00:21:04.805 --> 00:21:07.683
and, as it happens,
dairy cows grow horns.

00:21:07.683 --> 00:21:09.893
- And these horns
are a problem

00:21:09.893 --> 00:21:12.354
because they injure
other cattle and ranchers.

00:21:12.354 --> 00:21:15.482
- You can imagine if this was
a particularly aggressive bull,

00:21:15.482 --> 00:21:17.484
that he could
hurt his pen mates.

00:21:17.484 --> 00:21:20.112
- Instead of manually
sawing off these horns,

00:21:20.112 --> 00:21:22.197
Alison is breeding a dairy cow

00:21:22.197 --> 00:21:25.492
that doesn't grow them
in the first place.

00:21:25.492 --> 00:21:27.953
Working with a type of cattle
that has no horns,

00:21:27.953 --> 00:21:29.454
she had their hornless gene

00:21:29.454 --> 00:21:32.082
inserted into horned
dairy bull cells.

00:21:32.082 --> 00:21:35.669
These cells were cloned
to make the hornless animals.

00:21:35.669 --> 00:21:37.671
These hornless cows
are descendants

00:21:37.671 --> 00:21:40.257
of a gene-edited
hornless bull.

00:21:40.257 --> 00:21:42.426
- These are
proof of concept animals.

00:21:42.426 --> 00:21:44.720
They are kind of a prototype

00:21:44.720 --> 00:21:46.722
of how you could use
genome editing.

00:21:46.722 --> 00:21:49.266
- This genetic technique
produces offspring

00:21:49.266 --> 00:21:51.310
with a desired trait
much more quickly than

00:21:51.310 --> 00:21:53.645
the decades of breeding
normally required

00:21:53.645 --> 00:21:55.439
in traditional
animal husbandry.

00:21:55.439 --> 00:21:58.191
- That's really what
editing does for us,

00:21:58.191 --> 00:21:59.776
is it enables us to bring in

00:21:59.776 --> 00:22:02.321
one useful characteristic
that we want--

00:22:02.321 --> 00:22:04.656
in this case,
not growing horns--

00:22:04.656 --> 00:22:06.950
and not alter
the rest of the genetics.

00:22:06.950 --> 00:22:10.370
- This technique shows that
gene editing tools can be used

00:22:10.370 --> 00:22:15.208
to introduce desirable traits
from one animal into another.

00:22:15.208 --> 00:22:17.127
[light music]

00:22:17.127 --> 00:22:19.212
Outside of trying to
remove the horns,

00:22:19.212 --> 00:22:21.840
are there any other expressions

00:22:21.840 --> 00:22:25.260
that you would want to
get rid of or add?

00:22:25.260 --> 00:22:26.553
- So what else might we do?

00:22:26.553 --> 00:22:29.514
One of the targets that's
a really obvious one for

00:22:29.514 --> 00:22:32.684
plant and animal breeders
is disease resistance.

00:22:32.684 --> 00:22:34.311
And so globally it's estimated

00:22:34.311 --> 00:22:38.398
we lose about 20% of all
animal production to disease.

00:22:38.398 --> 00:22:40.025
- That's a large percent.

00:22:40.025 --> 00:22:42.444
That's hundreds of millions
of cattle

00:22:42.444 --> 00:22:44.988
needlessly lost every year.

00:22:44.988 --> 00:22:48.950
- To me, genetics is the best
approach to deal with disease,

00:22:48.950 --> 00:22:51.078
'cause if they don't get sick,
they don't need to be treated

00:22:51.078 --> 00:22:53.163
with antibiotics.
They're more productive.

00:22:53.163 --> 00:22:56.083
Farmer's happy, cattle are
happy, consumers are happy.

00:22:56.083 --> 00:22:59.336
So it's kind of a triple win
for sustainability.

00:22:59.336 --> 00:23:01.421
- But there's an even more
fundamental trait

00:23:01.421 --> 00:23:03.465
that Alison is
trying to select for.

00:23:03.465 --> 00:23:07.427
- In the beef cattle industry,
we would actually prefer males.

00:23:07.427 --> 00:23:10.555
- That's because male beef
cattle produce quantitatively

00:23:10.555 --> 00:23:13.225
more meat per pound of feed
than females.

00:23:13.225 --> 00:23:16.269
So Alison is also using
gene editing to breed cattle

00:23:16.269 --> 00:23:18.480
who only produce
male offspring.

00:23:18.480 --> 00:23:20.941
To achieve this,
she inserts a special gene

00:23:20.941 --> 00:23:23.235
into cow embryos in the lab.

00:23:23.235 --> 00:23:26.154
This makes selecting
the male sex possible.

00:23:26.154 --> 00:23:29.491
This could save more animals
from being slaughtered,

00:23:29.491 --> 00:23:30.992
and techniques like these

00:23:30.992 --> 00:23:33.495
could be used to
insert disease resistance,

00:23:33.495 --> 00:23:37.707
potentially reducing
the need for antibiotics.

00:23:37.707 --> 00:23:40.419
In fact, a gene-edited embryo

00:23:40.419 --> 00:23:43.338
was implanted into this cow
just three months ago.

00:23:43.338 --> 00:23:46.341
- And there is Princess.

00:23:46.341 --> 00:23:48.218
- So in a few short moments,

00:23:48.218 --> 00:23:52.305
we're about to see
an ultrasound of a cow here.

00:23:52.305 --> 00:23:53.849
Veterinarian Bret McNabb

00:23:53.849 --> 00:23:56.184
is going to perform
an ultrasound on Princess

00:23:56.184 --> 00:24:01.189
to see if the gene-edited
embryo has taken hold.

00:24:01.189 --> 00:24:03.233
- We're just gonna make sure
that the pregnancy

00:24:03.233 --> 00:24:05.485
is still healthy and viable
from what we can tell.

00:24:05.485 --> 00:24:07.279
- So an ultrasound on a cow

00:24:07.279 --> 00:24:09.197
is not quite the same
as an ultrasound on...

00:24:09.197 --> 00:24:10.449
[laughter]

00:24:10.449 --> 00:24:11.867
- The principles are the same,

00:24:11.867 --> 00:24:15.078
but our approach
is a little bit different.

00:24:15.078 --> 00:24:18.123
- Alison's checkup may depend
on the whims of this mama cow

00:24:18.123 --> 00:24:20.625
to get the ultrasound scanner
up her...

00:24:20.625 --> 00:24:22.794
well, you know.

00:24:22.794 --> 00:24:24.379
[quirky music]

00:24:24.379 --> 00:24:25.964
♪ ♪

00:24:25.964 --> 00:24:27.591
[cow lows]

00:24:27.591 --> 00:24:29.843
- [chuckles]
You're doing that next time.

00:24:29.843 --> 00:24:32.512
- You know, I'm learning quite
a bit just observing, so...

00:24:32.512 --> 00:24:35.682
- Oh, I see.
[laughter]

00:24:35.682 --> 00:24:37.642
- She's pregnant.
- That's good news.

00:24:37.642 --> 00:24:39.686
- It appears
the embryo implant

00:24:39.686 --> 00:24:41.938
has successfully taken hold.

00:24:41.938 --> 00:24:44.191
- So on ultrasound, you know,
we use sound waves,

00:24:44.191 --> 00:24:46.359
and so anything
that's more dense

00:24:46.359 --> 00:24:48.653
is gonna bounce the sound
wave back to my probe.

00:24:48.653 --> 00:24:51.198
And then you can start to see
floating around in there

00:24:51.198 --> 00:24:52.616
are those bright white
structures.

00:24:52.616 --> 00:24:54.951
Those are all
parts of the calf.

00:24:54.951 --> 00:24:56.745
- Oh, yeah.
Okay.

00:24:56.745 --> 00:24:58.580
- Based on certain structures,

00:24:58.580 --> 00:25:03.001
we can sex the calf,
and it looks like a male.

00:25:03.001 --> 00:25:06.546
- For Alison's endeavor to
breed male-only beef cattle,

00:25:06.546 --> 00:25:08.840
this is a significant
milestone.

00:25:08.840 --> 00:25:11.551
While these techniques
are still experimental

00:25:11.551 --> 00:25:13.720
and haven't yet been
approved by the FDA,

00:25:13.720 --> 00:25:16.139
Alison believes that
consuming products from

00:25:16.139 --> 00:25:20.185
gene edited cattle poses
no threat to human health.

00:25:20.185 --> 00:25:23.230
Artificially selecting
for the male sex could make

00:25:23.230 --> 00:25:26.441
cattle production more humane
and more efficient.

00:25:26.441 --> 00:25:28.527
- There are some
pretty compelling benefits

00:25:28.527 --> 00:25:31.571
that outweigh the risks.

00:25:31.571 --> 00:25:33.907
- Seeing the genetic
engineering of livestock

00:25:33.907 --> 00:25:37.869
up-close and personal
is absolutely mind-blowing.

00:25:37.869 --> 00:25:40.580
[bright music]

00:25:41.748 --> 00:25:45.001
- In the future, engineering
the genes of farm animals

00:25:45.001 --> 00:25:49.422
speeds up their evolution
as useful domestic species.

00:25:49.422 --> 00:25:53.385
A new variety of cow gene
edited to dramatically reduce

00:25:53.385 --> 00:25:56.638
the emission of the
climate-damaging gas methane

00:25:56.638 --> 00:26:00.016
is vastly reducing
global warming.

00:26:00.016 --> 00:26:02.811
Organs from genetically
modified pigs

00:26:02.811 --> 00:26:05.230
are safely implanted
into humans

00:26:05.230 --> 00:26:07.941
without fear of rejection
from the immune system.

00:26:07.941 --> 00:26:12.779
No one dies from a lack
of an organ donor anymore.

00:26:12.779 --> 00:26:14.573
[energetic music]

00:26:14.573 --> 00:26:17.075
- Humans have selectively
bred plants and animals,

00:26:17.075 --> 00:26:20.161
refining their traits,
for thousands of years.

00:26:20.161 --> 00:26:21.997
We've even created hybrids

00:26:21.997 --> 00:26:25.208
by mating creatures
from two different species.

00:26:25.208 --> 00:26:29.296
For example, a donkey
and a horse make a mule.

00:26:29.296 --> 00:26:31.339
But in the lab,
mixing up genetics

00:26:31.339 --> 00:26:35.176
can result in anything
and, well, everything.

00:26:35.176 --> 00:26:38.138
Jellyfish DNA
spliced into a bunny

00:26:38.138 --> 00:26:40.056
results in
a fluorescent bunny.

00:26:40.056 --> 00:26:42.934
When spider DNA
is edited into goats,

00:26:42.934 --> 00:26:45.604
their milk can be spun
into spider silk.

00:26:45.604 --> 00:26:47.522
Hybrids like these are
often bioengineered

00:26:47.522 --> 00:26:50.567
for research purposes.

00:26:50.567 --> 00:26:53.945
But one scientist is using
CRISPR's crossbreeding ability

00:26:53.945 --> 00:26:55.905
to do something
truly ambitious.

00:26:55.905 --> 00:26:59.701
He's bringing back the genes
of extinct species.

00:26:59.701 --> 00:27:01.995
I'm in Cambridge,
Massachusetts,

00:27:01.995 --> 00:27:03.371
to meet Dr. George Church,

00:27:03.371 --> 00:27:05.332
a legend in
the field of genetics,

00:27:05.332 --> 00:27:07.626
one of the originators
of gene engineering.

00:27:07.626 --> 00:27:09.836
He's been working in
Russian Siberia

00:27:09.836 --> 00:27:11.713
to find the remains of
woolly mammoths

00:27:11.713 --> 00:27:13.840
with the aim of
resurrecting their DNA

00:27:13.840 --> 00:27:16.217
to fight climate change.

00:27:16.217 --> 00:27:18.720
- There's unfortunately lots of
melting ice in Siberia.

00:27:18.720 --> 00:27:21.723
As there are millions of
mammoths that are frozen

00:27:21.723 --> 00:27:23.516
that are becoming exposed,

00:27:23.516 --> 00:27:28.063
we had access to six really
excellently frozen specimens.

00:27:28.063 --> 00:27:31.650
They had never thawed
in 40,000 years.

00:27:31.650 --> 00:27:32.942
- When you were
grabbing the samples

00:27:32.942 --> 00:27:35.737
from the woolly mammoth,
anatomically where...

00:27:35.737 --> 00:27:38.615
- We're dissecting
big chunks of

00:27:38.615 --> 00:27:41.368
mammoth legs with a drill bit,

00:27:41.368 --> 00:27:43.119
and we're kind of suited up

00:27:43.119 --> 00:27:46.247
because there's meat
flying all over the place.

00:27:47.415 --> 00:27:50.418
- Due to overhunting
and environmental changes,

00:27:50.418 --> 00:27:52.796
woolly mammoths
began going extinct

00:27:52.796 --> 00:27:55.173
around 10,000 years ago.

00:27:55.173 --> 00:27:57.634
But George is
extracting their DNA

00:27:57.634 --> 00:28:00.095
from the cold preserved
remains in Siberia

00:28:00.095 --> 00:28:02.138
and mapping their genome.

00:28:02.138 --> 00:28:04.224
- So we read the genome
into the computer

00:28:04.224 --> 00:28:08.478
and then we write it into
modern Asian elephant cells.

00:28:08.478 --> 00:28:11.356
- Modern Asian elephants
and woolly mammoths

00:28:11.356 --> 00:28:14.526
share common ancestry,
but are two distinct species.

00:28:14.526 --> 00:28:18.071
George is using advanced
CRISPR techniques to resurrect

00:28:18.071 --> 00:28:21.241
multiple cold resistant genes
from the woolly mammoth

00:28:21.241 --> 00:28:24.411
that grow extra hair
and produce more fat.

00:28:24.411 --> 00:28:27.247
He then plans to integrate
these genetic traits

00:28:27.247 --> 00:28:29.791
into the eggs of
Asian elephants.

00:28:29.791 --> 00:28:32.293
- We can make dozens of edits
to the genome

00:28:32.293 --> 00:28:35.505
and then clone them
into baby elephants.

00:28:35.505 --> 00:28:37.424
- But why?

00:28:37.424 --> 00:28:39.718
It turns out
cold-resistant elephants

00:28:39.718 --> 00:28:42.762
could also help mitigate
global warming.

00:28:42.762 --> 00:28:45.181
In the frigid tundra
of Siberia,

00:28:45.181 --> 00:28:47.892
grass is more effective
at keeping the Arctic cold

00:28:47.892 --> 00:28:49.436
than the current
forced environment,

00:28:49.436 --> 00:28:51.062
which retains heat.

00:28:51.062 --> 00:28:52.689
- Those millions of
square kilometers

00:28:52.689 --> 00:28:56.276
are at risk of warming,
and the only herbivore

00:28:56.276 --> 00:28:58.278
that can knock down the trees
is the elephant.

00:28:58.278 --> 00:29:01.865
Oddly, the herbivores can
change it back to grasslands,

00:29:01.865 --> 00:29:04.242
which is more photosynthetic.

00:29:04.242 --> 00:29:07.787
- A sizable population of
cold-resistant elephants

00:29:07.787 --> 00:29:09.497
would help maintain
this region

00:29:09.497 --> 00:29:12.083
as grasslands through grazing.

00:29:12.083 --> 00:29:14.043
And a more
photosynthetic Arctic

00:29:14.043 --> 00:29:16.504
would absorb
more carbon dioxide.

00:29:16.504 --> 00:29:18.256
- So it's part of
what will hopefully be

00:29:18.256 --> 00:29:21.092
a big international effort
to convert the Arctic,

00:29:21.092 --> 00:29:23.428
at least partially,
back to the form

00:29:23.428 --> 00:29:27.015
that was more conducive
to fighting climate change.

00:29:27.015 --> 00:29:30.852
- Is it outside of the realm
of possibility to, say,

00:29:30.852 --> 00:29:34.022
bring back an extinct creature
like a woolly mammoth?

00:29:34.022 --> 00:29:35.356
- Once we get that good at it,

00:29:35.356 --> 00:29:38.193
we may switch
the cold-resistant elephants

00:29:38.193 --> 00:29:40.779
into fully genetically
identical mammoths.

00:29:40.779 --> 00:29:43.281
- Who knew resurrecting
woolly mammoth DNA

00:29:43.281 --> 00:29:47.035
could help restore icy
conditions across the Arctic?

00:29:49.078 --> 00:29:51.581
- In the future,
genes from most creatures

00:29:51.581 --> 00:29:54.626
can be safely inserted
into other species

00:29:54.626 --> 00:29:57.378
to create
revolutionary hybrids.

00:29:57.378 --> 00:29:59.672
Engineered jellyfish
with genes

00:29:59.672 --> 00:30:03.218
from plastic-eating microbes
now clean up the oceans

00:30:03.218 --> 00:30:06.763
by organically breaking down
non-decomposing trash.

00:30:06.763 --> 00:30:10.099
Some scientists have
even extracted DNA

00:30:10.099 --> 00:30:12.852
from dinosaur remains
and are on the brink

00:30:12.852 --> 00:30:16.272
of resurrecting
these extinct species.

00:30:16.272 --> 00:30:19.984
The hope is by bringing a
single dinosaur back to life,

00:30:19.984 --> 00:30:22.028
new technologies
will be developed

00:30:22.028 --> 00:30:25.615
to help other species
on the brink of extinction.

00:30:28.451 --> 00:30:30.495
- Speaking with
George Church firsthand

00:30:30.495 --> 00:30:33.081
is both humbling
and inspiring.

00:30:33.081 --> 00:30:34.749
As if resurrecting the DNA

00:30:34.749 --> 00:30:36.459
of the woolly mammoth
wasn't enough,

00:30:36.459 --> 00:30:41.548
he also helped start the $3
billion Human Genome Project.

00:30:41.548 --> 00:30:45.552
This landmark study identified
every base pair of DNA

00:30:45.552 --> 00:30:48.596
and mapped the entire
human genome.

00:30:48.596 --> 00:30:51.599
Its primary purpose
was to conquer disease.

00:30:51.599 --> 00:30:55.436
It was and remains
a really big deal.

00:30:55.436 --> 00:30:57.230
- We are here
to celebrate the completion

00:30:57.230 --> 00:31:00.441
of the first survey
of the entire human genome.

00:31:00.441 --> 00:31:03.194
- This roadmap of
the intricate genetic codes

00:31:03.194 --> 00:31:04.779
across the human body

00:31:04.779 --> 00:31:08.616
empowers doctors to better
diagnose and treat disease.

00:31:08.616 --> 00:31:11.077
- We've worked really hard
on bringing down the costs

00:31:11.077 --> 00:31:13.037
of reading genomes,
which I think is a thing that

00:31:13.037 --> 00:31:14.998
most people
could benefit from.

00:31:14.998 --> 00:31:18.334
- Thanks to advances in
DNA sequencing technology,

00:31:18.334 --> 00:31:22.005
the cost of reading
entire genomes has plummeted.

00:31:22.005 --> 00:31:24.924
- All it would take is
a small tipping point event

00:31:24.924 --> 00:31:27.260
to shift it over so that
everybody's using this.

00:31:27.260 --> 00:31:28.595
And in the case
of genetics,

00:31:28.595 --> 00:31:30.430
that would be reading
everybody's genome

00:31:30.430 --> 00:31:33.850
and giving them information
that was actionable.

00:31:33.850 --> 00:31:36.895
- By actionable, he means
letting individuals know

00:31:36.895 --> 00:31:40.565
what kinds of diseases they're
genetically susceptible to.

00:31:40.565 --> 00:31:42.817
This empowers people
to be proactive

00:31:42.817 --> 00:31:45.778
in avoiding or managing
imminent diseases.

00:31:45.778 --> 00:31:47.322
- I think
we're on a trajectory

00:31:47.322 --> 00:31:49.240
where everybody in the world
are gonna get sequenced

00:31:49.240 --> 00:31:50.867
within a few years.

00:31:50.867 --> 00:31:53.828
- The Human Genome Project
continues to have a big impact

00:31:53.828 --> 00:31:56.998
in treating all kinds
of human diseases.

00:31:58.249 --> 00:31:59.834
[light dramatic music]

00:31:59.834 --> 00:32:01.920
To find out how,
I'm at the historic

00:32:01.920 --> 00:32:04.881
Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory
in Long Island, New York,

00:32:04.881 --> 00:32:07.634
to meet with
Dr. Bruce Stillman.

00:32:07.634 --> 00:32:09.302
- There has been a revolution

00:32:09.302 --> 00:32:11.387
coming from
the Human Genome Project

00:32:11.387 --> 00:32:13.932
and a lot of cancer research
is linked to that.

00:32:13.932 --> 00:32:18.353
We now have a very deep
understanding of that genetics

00:32:18.353 --> 00:32:22.231
and what that can do is
to link new therapeutics

00:32:22.231 --> 00:32:26.027
to individual patient's
genetics.

00:32:26.027 --> 00:32:28.363
- I have an interest
in oncology.

00:32:28.363 --> 00:32:29.697
- Mm-hmm.

00:32:29.697 --> 00:32:32.075
- I lost an aunt
to breast cancer,

00:32:32.075 --> 00:32:33.868
and I think that took me
down this journey

00:32:33.868 --> 00:32:35.411
towards finding a solution.

00:32:35.411 --> 00:32:38.706
- One of our scientists
used a very interesting

00:32:38.706 --> 00:32:41.000
genetic technique of
genetic selection,

00:32:41.000 --> 00:32:45.213
and now we're gearing up
to use that information

00:32:45.213 --> 00:32:48.049
to improve cancer therapy.

00:32:48.049 --> 00:32:49.884
- Founded in 1890,

00:32:49.884 --> 00:32:52.845
this lab is home to
eight Nobel Prize winners.

00:32:52.845 --> 00:32:54.681
- So this was the first lab

00:32:54.681 --> 00:32:56.891
at Cold Spring Harbor,
and still used.

00:32:56.891 --> 00:32:58.309
This is a cancer laboratory,

00:32:58.309 --> 00:33:00.895
so still used
for cancer research.

00:33:00.895 --> 00:33:03.189
- Scientists here are using
gene editing techniques,

00:33:03.189 --> 00:33:06.651
including CRISPR, to develop
new ways to fight cancer.

00:33:06.651 --> 00:33:08.778
These advances are
leading to treatments,

00:33:08.778 --> 00:33:12.657
tailored to work with an
individual's unique genetics.

00:33:12.657 --> 00:33:14.826
- So for instance,
if you have a mutation

00:33:14.826 --> 00:33:17.787
in a particular gene
that causes lung cancer,

00:33:17.787 --> 00:33:22.000
there is a therapeutic that is
targeted to that lung cancer.

00:33:22.000 --> 00:33:23.876
- But in the shadow
of its long history

00:33:23.876 --> 00:33:26.587
of using genetics
to improve human life,

00:33:26.587 --> 00:33:28.006
Cold Spring Harbor Labs

00:33:28.006 --> 00:33:31.843
do have an unfortunate
and dark past.

00:33:31.843 --> 00:33:35.930
- What happened in
the 1910s and the 1920s

00:33:35.930 --> 00:33:38.516
was that scientists
began to believe

00:33:38.516 --> 00:33:43.396
that a lot of traits were
inherited by individual genes,

00:33:43.396 --> 00:33:45.481
when in fact they weren't.

00:33:45.481 --> 00:33:48.276
- This led to an era of
what's called eugenics--

00:33:48.276 --> 00:33:52.113
that is, the selective
breeding of humans.

00:33:52.113 --> 00:33:54.323
Cold Spring Harbor Labs
even opened

00:33:54.323 --> 00:33:57.910
a eugenics records office to
gather biological information

00:33:57.910 --> 00:34:00.830
on the American population.

00:34:00.830 --> 00:34:03.124
At the time,
people of certain traits

00:34:03.124 --> 00:34:05.251
that some believed
to be desirable

00:34:05.251 --> 00:34:07.420
were deemed fit to reproduce,

00:34:07.420 --> 00:34:09.881
while minorities and
those with disabilities

00:34:09.881 --> 00:34:12.925
were blocked from marrying
and were even sterilized.

00:34:12.925 --> 00:34:18.639
- That eugenics movement
got way off track from science.

00:34:18.639 --> 00:34:23.019
Scientists pushed back against
this eugenics movement,

00:34:23.019 --> 00:34:25.730
and, by the 1930s,

00:34:25.730 --> 00:34:29.859
it was effectively shut down
in the United States.

00:34:29.859 --> 00:34:32.070
- Despite this skeleton
in the closet,

00:34:32.070 --> 00:34:34.155
it's important
to know our history

00:34:34.155 --> 00:34:35.948
so as not to repeat it.

00:34:35.948 --> 00:34:38.785
And from what I see at
Cold Spring Harbor Labs today,

00:34:38.785 --> 00:34:42.205
I think there's plenty of room
to be optimistic.

00:34:42.205 --> 00:34:44.290
When real science
is conducted,

00:34:44.290 --> 00:34:46.334
true progress can be made.

00:34:46.334 --> 00:34:50.379
And the best place to start
is with affected children.

00:34:50.379 --> 00:34:54.717
- We have worked on a disease
called spinal muscular atrophy,

00:34:54.717 --> 00:34:57.887
which is a mutation
that children inherit.

00:34:57.887 --> 00:35:00.014
The child will eventually die.

00:35:00.014 --> 00:35:02.058
But the laboratory
developed a drug,

00:35:02.058 --> 00:35:04.977
which actually prevents
these children from dying

00:35:04.977 --> 00:35:08.064
and actually gives them
a fairly high quality of life.

00:35:08.064 --> 00:35:10.399
- All this makes me think of
young Annabel Frost

00:35:10.399 --> 00:35:13.027
and her debilitating
genetic disorder.

00:35:13.027 --> 00:35:16.239
And this kind of research
fills me with hope.

00:35:16.239 --> 00:35:19.492
In fact, genetic engineering
can even remedy

00:35:19.492 --> 00:35:22.286
genetic disorders
like sickle cell anemia.

00:35:22.286 --> 00:35:25.748
Caused by a mutation, this
disease deforms blood cells

00:35:25.748 --> 00:35:28.709
into hook-like shapes,
which can stick together

00:35:28.709 --> 00:35:31.712
and cause life-threatening
blood clots.

00:35:31.712 --> 00:35:34.674
- CRISPR's now being
used to change genes

00:35:34.674 --> 00:35:37.093
in sickle cell anemia,

00:35:37.093 --> 00:35:40.221
where you then transplant
back into those patients

00:35:40.221 --> 00:35:42.974
cells in the blood system

00:35:42.974 --> 00:35:47.228
that will essentially reverse
the sickle cell disease.

00:35:47.228 --> 00:35:50.690
And so those types of trials
are occurring now with CRISPR.

00:35:50.690 --> 00:35:53.442
- Recently,
scientists gene edited

00:35:53.442 --> 00:35:56.946
a patient's own stem cells
that produce bone marrow.

00:35:56.946 --> 00:35:58.823
They then reinjected

00:35:58.823 --> 00:36:01.450
these altered cells
back into her body.

00:36:01.450 --> 00:36:04.412
Amazingly, this therapy
cured the patient's

00:36:04.412 --> 00:36:07.665
sickle cell disease
for the first time in history.

00:36:07.665 --> 00:36:10.710
- Technologies like CRISPR
are incredibly powerful

00:36:10.710 --> 00:36:13.337
and can change the world.

00:36:13.337 --> 00:36:15.173
They also have the potential

00:36:15.173 --> 00:36:19.051
for changing humanity
as itself.

00:36:19.051 --> 00:36:21.012
- But there are limitations.

00:36:21.012 --> 00:36:24.098
The CRISPR technology
is not perfect,

00:36:24.098 --> 00:36:25.850
and sometimes
it makes mistakes

00:36:25.850 --> 00:36:29.187
in the genetic reworking
that could be catastrophic.

00:36:29.187 --> 00:36:31.314
This means CRISPR's
implementation

00:36:31.314 --> 00:36:35.193
directly into humans
remains a risky prospect.

00:36:35.193 --> 00:36:37.320
But one visionary scientist
is aiming

00:36:37.320 --> 00:36:39.071
to give gene editing
techniques

00:36:39.071 --> 00:36:41.157
an even higher level
of precision.

00:36:41.157 --> 00:36:46.245
Dr. David Liu of the Broad
Institute of Harvard and MIT

00:36:46.245 --> 00:36:48.748
is working on tools and
techniques that may one day

00:36:48.748 --> 00:36:52.877
lead to the safe
gene editing of humans.

00:36:52.877 --> 00:36:54.253
He's developing

00:36:54.253 --> 00:36:58.049
a new gene editing tool
called prime editing.

00:36:58.049 --> 00:37:01.135
Can you tell me a little bit
about your work in that regard?

00:37:01.135 --> 00:37:03.679
- So the machines
that nature provides us,

00:37:03.679 --> 00:37:05.348
like CRISPR-Cas9,

00:37:05.348 --> 00:37:09.143
often don't do
what we want them to do.

00:37:09.143 --> 00:37:11.729
The results of breaking
that double helix

00:37:11.729 --> 00:37:14.065
most frequently
disrupts things

00:37:14.065 --> 00:37:17.235
to cause the deletion
or insertion of small numbers

00:37:17.235 --> 00:37:21.113
of DNA letters at the cut site.

00:37:21.113 --> 00:37:23.741
- Both CRISPR and prime
editing technologies

00:37:23.741 --> 00:37:25.826
work by cutting DNA.

00:37:25.826 --> 00:37:28.246
But in some sensitive
situations,

00:37:28.246 --> 00:37:31.249
CRISPR technology can be
too blunt of a tool.

00:37:31.249 --> 00:37:34.835
That's because it breaks both
strands of the double helix.

00:37:34.835 --> 00:37:37.588
In rare instances,
this can disrupt the gene

00:37:37.588 --> 00:37:39.423
in unintended ways.

00:37:39.423 --> 00:37:42.426
But prime editing is
more like a pair of tweezers.

00:37:42.426 --> 00:37:45.054
It breaks only one strand
of the double helix,

00:37:45.054 --> 00:37:47.556
allowing scientists to
very precisely change

00:37:47.556 --> 00:37:49.225
a single base pair.

00:37:49.225 --> 00:37:52.186
This is like changing one note
in a musical score.

00:37:52.186 --> 00:37:55.398
- For most diseases
with a genetic component,

00:37:55.398 --> 00:37:59.652
it's believed that in order
to treat the disease,

00:37:59.652 --> 00:38:02.822
you need to precisely
change that mutated gene

00:38:02.822 --> 00:38:06.242
back to the normal
DNA sequence.

00:38:06.242 --> 00:38:09.787
- This ultra-precise technique
means David can replace

00:38:09.787 --> 00:38:13.791
an individual mutation
on a single step of DNA

00:38:13.791 --> 00:38:17.003
and not an entire section
of the ladder.

00:38:17.003 --> 00:38:20.715
- So you can make those kinds
of changes using prime editors,

00:38:20.715 --> 00:38:24.844
the kinds of changes that we
believe could directly correct

00:38:24.844 --> 00:38:27.930
genetic disease-causing
mutations.

00:38:27.930 --> 00:38:31.142
- In the lab, David has
successfully corrected

00:38:31.142 --> 00:38:32.893
mutations for
genetic diseases,

00:38:32.893 --> 00:38:36.439
including Tay-Sachs
and cystic fibrosis.

00:38:36.439 --> 00:38:39.525
And he thinks this prime
technology will soon be ready

00:38:39.525 --> 00:38:43.738
to help people with genetic
disorders, like Annabel.

00:38:43.738 --> 00:38:48.534
How far off would you say is
a potential human application?

00:38:48.534 --> 00:38:52.163
- We should have some
of the first drugs ready

00:38:52.163 --> 00:38:56.500
by perhaps as early as within
the next five or ten years.

00:38:56.500 --> 00:38:58.502
It's an incredibly
exciting time.

00:38:58.502 --> 00:39:01.797
If you had asked me
about five or ten years ago,

00:39:01.797 --> 00:39:03.049
I would have said

00:39:03.049 --> 00:39:05.259
it still was in the realm
of science fiction.

00:39:05.259 --> 00:39:07.345
- Yeah, that's wonderful.

00:39:07.345 --> 00:39:10.723
This experimental technique
will become science fact,

00:39:10.723 --> 00:39:14.685
giving affected individuals
the prospect of a better life.

00:39:14.685 --> 00:39:16.437
But for now,

00:39:16.437 --> 00:39:20.316
reworking the genetic codes of
people remains controversial.

00:39:20.316 --> 00:39:23.277
In 2018,
a researcher stunned the world

00:39:23.277 --> 00:39:26.864
by using CRISPR
to gene edit HIV resistance

00:39:26.864 --> 00:39:30.117
into the embryos
of a pair of twins.

00:39:30.117 --> 00:39:32.828
Called germline editing,
this technique on humans

00:39:32.828 --> 00:39:34.455
was considered premature

00:39:34.455 --> 00:39:38.209
and was widely condemned
in the scientific community.

00:39:38.209 --> 00:39:40.711
- The first gene editing
in humans

00:39:40.711 --> 00:39:42.505
was a profound
misuse of science

00:39:42.505 --> 00:39:46.175
with profound implications
in society in general.

00:39:46.175 --> 00:39:48.094
- The involved parties
were punished

00:39:48.094 --> 00:39:51.389
for disregarding
safety regulations.

00:39:51.389 --> 00:39:54.767
Fortunately or unfortunately,
the Pandora's box

00:39:54.767 --> 00:39:58.396
of genetic engineering
has been thrown wide-open.

00:39:58.396 --> 00:40:03.234
- In many ways, this is one
of the costs of doing science.

00:40:03.234 --> 00:40:05.486
The technology itself
is agnostic.

00:40:05.486 --> 00:40:06.904
It's neither good nor bad.

00:40:06.904 --> 00:40:10.324
The real question is,
"What do people do

00:40:10.324 --> 00:40:14.829
"with that technology and how
careful and/or mindful are they

00:40:14.829 --> 00:40:18.457
to the potential unintended
consequences that we have?"

00:40:18.457 --> 00:40:20.543
- With the depth of control
over evolution

00:40:20.543 --> 00:40:22.711
that gene editing enables

00:40:22.711 --> 00:40:26.382
in breeding plants, animals,
and eventually humans,

00:40:26.382 --> 00:40:29.760
we are entering uncharted
waters as a species.

00:40:29.760 --> 00:40:32.012
It's difficult
to draw a hard line

00:40:32.012 --> 00:40:35.099
between where progress
should stop

00:40:35.099 --> 00:40:40.187
and where our morality
or our ethics should come in.

00:40:40.187 --> 00:40:42.189
Some might argue that

00:40:42.189 --> 00:40:45.901
some lines have to be crossed
to make progress.

00:40:48.571 --> 00:40:50.531
And there has been
much progress,

00:40:50.531 --> 00:40:52.241
especially in agriculture.

00:40:52.241 --> 00:40:55.453
Today, more than 90%
of corn and soybeans

00:40:55.453 --> 00:40:57.913
are genetically modified,
or GMO.

00:40:57.913 --> 00:41:02.126
These crops require less
pesticide, land, and water.

00:41:02.126 --> 00:41:05.004
Scientists are also using
gene editing to develop crops

00:41:05.004 --> 00:41:07.923
that are more nutritious
and even drought-resistant.

00:41:07.923 --> 00:41:10.384
Though many in the public
remain skeptical,

00:41:10.384 --> 00:41:12.678
the vast majority of
scientists believe

00:41:12.678 --> 00:41:14.597
GMO foods are safe.

00:41:14.597 --> 00:41:19.018
- Tinkering with genes for
benefit and for good purpose

00:41:19.018 --> 00:41:22.313
is actually
an ethical thing to do.

00:41:22.313 --> 00:41:24.315
- And as for humans,
many believe

00:41:24.315 --> 00:41:27.485
the benefits of gene editing
outweigh the risks.

00:41:27.485 --> 00:41:30.196
- There are 10,000
monogenic diseases affecting

00:41:30.196 --> 00:41:33.032
hundreds of millions of
children across the world.

00:41:33.032 --> 00:41:34.992
Precise medicine would help,

00:41:34.992 --> 00:41:38.496
and then we start
building on that.

00:41:38.496 --> 00:41:41.248
- In the right hands,
I think genetic engineering

00:41:41.248 --> 00:41:42.875
will usher in
a much better future

00:41:42.875 --> 00:41:45.961
for people and our planet.

00:41:45.961 --> 00:41:48.172
Some think it will even shift

00:41:48.172 --> 00:41:50.925
human evolution
into overdrive.

00:41:50.925 --> 00:41:54.470
- In 30 years,
we may be unrecognizable.

00:41:54.470 --> 00:41:56.180
I don't think that's
going to be our intention,

00:41:56.180 --> 00:41:58.224
but when you take
these big leaps

00:41:58.224 --> 00:42:02.102
and get comfortable with them,
people get addicted.

00:42:02.102 --> 00:42:04.355
- I'm hopeful that
gene editing technology

00:42:04.355 --> 00:42:07.858
will ultimately help people
like my aunt and Annabel

00:42:07.858 --> 00:42:09.985
and, for those reasons
and many more,

00:42:09.985 --> 00:42:12.530
I believe the rise of
genetic engineering

00:42:12.530 --> 00:42:15.282
can't come fast enough.

